IQ and SAT tests measure convergent thinking (single correct answers) but not divergent thinking, creativity, emotional intelligence, or practical intelligence — forms of ability that predict real-world success.
Howard Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences identifies 8 distinct cognitive abilities; standardised tests measure at most 2–3, leaving musical, spatial, bodily-kinaesthetic, and interpersonal intelligences entirely unassessed.
Correlation between SAT scores and college GPA is only r=0.35–0.46, meaning SAT scores explain at most 20% of variance in academic performance — weaker than high school GPA as a predictor.
